# Chapter 16: Transgenic animals

=== layout: subsection

# Topic 31: GMO mosquito

=== layout: one-panel

# Topic 31: GMO mosquito

@body

- If we change mosquitoes, can we reduce the diseases they transmit?
- Anopheles transmit malaria parasites; Aedes species transmit several important arboviruses.
- Some approaches suppress the mosquito population. Others reduce pathogen transmission.
- Self-limiting transgenes, Wolbachia, and gene drives work differently. Keep them separate.

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. GM Mosquitos

@body

- Talk about GM mosquitos and their role with malaria and Zika virus.
- How do we prevent the GM mosquitos from spreading?
- What types of GM mosquitos have been released in the world to date?
- What is the future of GM mosquitos hold?
- Do mosquitos have any ecological importances?

=== layout: two-panels

# 31. Gene drive: a 2018 cage experiment

@left

- A gene drive targeted the doublesex gene.
- Females with two disrupted copies had altered development and were sterile.
- This was a contained laboratory study.

[Kyrou et al., 2018](https://www.nature.com/articles/nbt.4245)

@right

![Male and female mosquito phenotypes](assets/lect06c-set_4_tp_chapter_12_16_material/image_074.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 31. Gene drive spread and cage suppression

@left

![Drive frequency and egg production by generation](assets/lect06c-set_4_tp_chapter_12_16_material/image_075.png)

@right

- Each cage began with 300 wild-type females, 150 wild-type males, and 150 GM males.
- Drive prevalence reached 100% in generations 7 and 11.
- Egg production collapsed in generations 8 and 12.
- Compare the two trajectories; the next slide enlarges them.

